# MODULE.bazel.lock is ignored for now as per this recommendation:
# https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazel/issues/20369
MODULE.bazel.lock

# Rust / Cargo build artifacts
#
# The ROOT Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT ignored.  This tree ships a system
# library, so that lock file is committed to keep every build of it reproducible.
# Do not add an ignore rule for it here.
#
# The same applies to fuzz/Cargo.lock.  fuzz/ is its own workspace root and so
# gets a lock file of its own, and it is tracked for the same reason: the five
# fuzz targets are a CI gate with a time budget, and a gate whose dependency
# versions can drift underneath it is measuring a different program each run.
# Leaving it untracked and unignored is the one option that does not work -- the
# first `cargo fuzz` invocation would write an untracked file into the tree, and
# every run after that would have to choose between `--locked` failing and a
# dirty working tree.  See the "WHAT IS NEVER COMMITTED, AND WHAT IS" section of
# fuzz/Cargo.toml.
#
# The same applies a third time to benches/Cargo.lock.  benches/ is excluded from
# the root workspace and is its own workspace root, because criterion 0.8.2 declares
# rust-version 1.86 and reaches edition-2024 clap manifests that cargo 1.80 -- this
# project's declared floor -- cannot parse at all; hosting the suites inside the
# workspace made `cargo +1.80 metadata --locked` and `cargo +1.80 check --locked
# --workspace --all-targets` fail outright.  Its lock is tracked because the
# throughput and memory gates are only meaningful against a fixed sampler.  See
# section 2 of benches/Cargo.toml.
#
# Cargo writes every product of the root workspace -- the three crates under
# crates/ and their test binaries -- into the one target/ directory at the top of
# the tree, so a single anchored rule covers the whole library build.
/target/
# fuzz/ is excluded from the root workspace and is its own workspace root, so
# cargo gives it a target/ of its own that the anchored rule above cannot reach.
fuzz/target/
# benches/ is excluded for the same structural reason and gets the same treatment:
# `cargo bench --manifest-path benches/Cargo.toml` writes benches/target/, which
# includes criterion's own reports under benches/target/criterion/.  Runtime
# measurement output, never source.
benches/target/
# Being its own workspace root, fuzz/ resolves independently and cargo writes a
# second lock file there.  That one IS committed, exactly like the root
# Cargo.lock: fuzz/deny.toml audits the harness graph against it and both
# documented invocations pass `--locked`, so it is a reproducibility surface and
# not build output.  There is deliberately no ignore rule for it.
# libFuzzer and cargo-fuzz scratch: crash and timeout reproducers, the corpus the
# fuzzer grows for itself, and the profile data `cargo fuzz coverage' writes to
# fuzz/coverage/<target>/ -- coverage.profdata plus the raw/ profiles it is merged
# from.  Together with fuzz/target/ above, those are every directory cargo-fuzz
# 0.13.2 creates inside fuzz/, which is the point: no documented subcommand of it
# may leave an untracked file in the tree, for exactly the reason the
# fuzz/Cargo.lock note above gives for `cargo fuzz run'.  `cargo fuzz coverage' is
# how the boundary coverage of each target is evidenced, so it gets run, and it is
# output rather than input -- regenerated from the corpus whenever it is wanted.
# All three rules stay anchored to fuzz/ on purpose -- a bare corpus/ would also
# swallow crates/zlib-rs-differential/corpus/, which is a committed, deterministic
# test fixture and has to stay tracked.
fuzz/artifacts/
fuzz/corpus/
fuzz/coverage/
# rustfmt backups.
**/*.rs.bk
# Scratch that the ABI verification steps write into the repository root: the
# cbindgen-generated header that `make rust-header' regenerates and gates, and
# the symbol lists that `make rust-symbols' compares and reports from.
# generated_zlib.h especially must never be committed -- a second header sitting
# beside the immutable zlib.h would be indistinguishable from the real public
# contract.  Three of these survive a passing run on purpose, because they are
# what the run produced: generated_zlib.h is the gated artifact itself, and
# rust.syms/c.syms are the two sorted lists `rust-symbols' reports its verdict
# from, so they stay readable afterwards.  The rest are transient -- a passing
# run deletes them, a FAILING `rust-header' keeps them because they are its
# evidence -- and `make rust-clean' removes all ten.  These rules cover the
# interrupted run, where anything may be left behind.
/generated_zlib.h
/generated_zlib.h.warn
/generated_zlib.h.log
/generated_zlib.h.probe.c
/generated_zlib.h.ref
/generated_zlib.h.gen
/rust.syms
/c.syms
/missing.syms
/extra.syms

# Bytecode from the benchmark gate.  `.github/scripts/bench_gate.py` is compiled
# with `python3 -m py_compile` before it is trusted -- a gate that cannot even be
# compiled must fail at setup rather than six steps later -- and its self-test
# imports nothing, but py_compile still writes a __pycache__ beside the source.
# Build output, never source, and the rule is unanchored so it covers the
# self-test's cache too.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
